# Process Management in Linux

## What is Process Management?

A process is a program that is currently running on a Linux system.

Process Management is the practice of monitoring, controlling, and managing running processes to ensure system stability, performance, and security.

---

## Why is Process Management Important?

Without Process Management

* Applications may consume excessive CPU or memory.
* Malicious processes may remain undetected.
* Systems may become slow or unstable.
* Troubleshooting becomes difficult.

With Process Management

* System performance can be monitored.
* Suspicious processes can be identified.
* Resource usage can be controlled.
* Security incidents can be investigated efficiently.

---

## Real-World Example

When you open Google Chrome:

```text
Google Chrome
      │
      ▼
 Linux Kernel
      │
      ▼
 Running Process
      │
      ▼
 CPU + Memory Usage
```

Linux assigns a Process ID (PID) to manage the application.

---

## What is a PID?

PID stands for Process ID.

Every running process receives a unique numeric identifier.

Example:

```text
PID     Process
1001    chrome
1205    firefox
1500    sshd
```

---

## View Running Processes

### ps

Displays currently running processes.

```bash
ps
```

Example Output:

```text
PID   TTY   TIME   CMD
1234  pts/0 00:00 bash
2345  pts/0 00:01 firefox
```

---

## View All Processes

```bash
ps -e
```

Displays every running process.

---

## Detailed Process Information

```bash
ps -ef
```

Example Output:

```text
UID   PID  PPID  CMD
root    1     0  systemd
user 1200     1  bash
```

Useful for incident response investigations.

---

## Search for Specific Processes

### grep

```bash
ps -ef | grep ssh
```

Example:

```bash
ps -ef | grep apache
```

Displays matching processes.

---

## Real-Time Process Monitoring

### top

Displays real-time system activity.

```bash
top
```

Shows:

* CPU usage
* Memory usage
* Running processes
* System load

Widely used by administrators.

---

## Understanding Top Output

Example:

```text
PID USER  CPU% MEM% COMMAND
245 root  15.2 5.1 apache2
```

Meaning:

* Process ID
* Owner
* CPU Usage
* Memory Usage
* Command Name

---

## Enhanced Process Monitoring

### htop

User-friendly alternative to top.

```bash
htop
```

Features:

* Color interface
* Process tree view
* Easier navigation
* Interactive process control

Installation:

```bash
sudo apt install htop
```

---

## View Process Tree

### pstree

Displays parent-child process relationships.

```bash
pstree
```

Example:

```text
systemd
 ├─sshd
 ├─NetworkManager
 └─bash
     └─firefox
```

Useful during investigations.

---

## Background Processes

Linux allows programs to run in the background.

Example:

```bash
firefox &
```

The ampersand (&) runs the process in the background.

---

## View Background Jobs

### jobs

```bash
jobs
```

Example Output:

```text
[1]+ Running firefox &
```

Displays active jobs.

---

## Move Process to Background

### bg

```bash
bg
```

Resumes a suspended job in the background.

---

## Move Process to Foreground

### fg

```bash
fg
```

Brings a background job to the foreground.

---

## Identify Process ID

### pidof

```bash
pidof firefox
```

Example Output:

```text
2450
```

Returns the process PID.

---

## Kill a Process

### kill

Terminates a process using its PID.

```bash
kill 2450
```

Example:

```bash
kill 1234
```

---

## Force Kill a Process

### kill -9

```bash
kill -9 2450
```

Uses the SIGKILL signal.

Forcefully terminates the process.

⚠ Use only when necessary.

---

## Kill by Process Name

### pkill

```bash
pkill firefox
```

Terminates processes by name.

---

## Kill All Matching Processes

### killall

```bash
killall firefox
```

Stops all processes with the specified name.

---

## Process Priority

Linux assigns priorities to processes.

Higher priority processes receive more CPU resources.

---

## View Process Priorities

```bash
top
```

Look for:

```text
NI = Nice Value
PR = Priority
```

---

## Start Process with Custom Priority

### nice

```bash
nice -n 10 firefox
```

Starts a process with lower priority.

---

## Change Running Process Priority

### renice

```bash
sudo renice 5 -p 2450
```

Changes priority of a running process.

---

## View System Services

### systemctl

Displays and manages services.

```bash
systemctl
```

---

## Check Service Status

```bash
systemctl status ssh
```

Example:

```bash
systemctl status apache2
```

Displays:

* Service state
* PID
* Logs
* Startup information

---

## Start a Service

```bash
sudo systemctl start apache2
```

---

## Stop a Service

```bash
sudo systemctl stop apache2
```

---

## Restart a Service

```bash
sudo systemctl restart apache2
```

---

## Enable Service at Boot

```bash
sudo systemctl enable apache2
```

---

## Disable Service at Boot

```bash
sudo systemctl disable apache2
```

---

## Why Process Management Matters in Cybersecurity

Security professionals use process management to:

* Detect malware
* Identify suspicious processes
* Investigate incidents
* Monitor system resources
* Stop malicious applications
* Analyze persistence mechanisms

Many attacks create unauthorized processes that can be identified through process analysis.

---

